A||||4||Eric||An 84???||||14||
Z||000000||Eric||05-27-2000||12:39 AM||||I've found a non running 84 Fiero for a good price of which I will not divulge at this time.  I have many doubts and concerns about owning a pre 88 Fiero especially one in this state.  I know most of you love your pre 88 Fieros, and even go so far as saying the handling is exceptional.  So I ask to those of you who have owned or driven both varieties, what's the difference.  I mean what's the difference in "feel" not in mechanical parts.  I've grown rather fond of my 88, but my driving needs are such that I spend, in my opinion, too much money on gas.  And the more I drive this particular car, the less it's worth and the more it costs me to repare the "exclusive" 88 parts.  In other words, I don't really want my 88 to be a daily driver.  The 84 needs extensive work which I am prepared to do.  It will also need a new engine (could be swap time, but I'd like to keep it a four cylinder, maybe a Quad 4).  What's the manual transmission for the 84?  Would it be worth retaining even with high miles?  The more I think about it, the more I get excited.  It would be nice to sink my teeth into another Fiero project with my 88 3.2 saga drawing to a close.  The greatest thing is I would be able to do more to this car and it would cost far less to do it.  So what do you all think?||||reg||14
Z||000001||Motorhead||05-27-2000||09:09 AM||||I own two pre'88 coupes and love them both.  One is an '86, the other an '84.  The 4 bangers are a bit underated in my opinion.  The most obvious advantage is gas mileage.  $10.00 every two weeks is nothing to sneeze at, I drive 14 miles a day back and forth to work.<BR>Parts are CHEAP and readily available.  <BR>For some reason the '84 seems to handle a little better than the '86.  Probably my imagination though.<BR>Rebuild costs are also much much cheaper.  DUHH.<BR>So go for it!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||05-27-2000||12:45 PM||||I own an 86 and have driven a few unmodified 88's. The handleing with the 88's "feeling" wise to me appears that the car feels more stable when going around banks and corners. The ride is slightly stiffer then the earlier ones and steering effort is slightly better. Now to make the 84 you want to buy handle and steer very close all you need to do is poly bushings, decent shocks and struts, and a rear swaybar. The 84's also came with a 4:10 ratio Muncie 4spd for the manual trans. Not a bad trans, the 5spd will get better MPG due to the lower gear ratio but acceleration is less. The 4cyl in the 84's can be rebuilt to get HP to about 110-115 without getting crazy. Originally the engine in there now is 92HP. If you do a quad4 swap it would be help performance but I would then convert to the 5spd for the gearing of higher MPG. If you have the time and money to do these things and with the vast experience of the forum members, you can make a very nice daily driver. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||05-28-2000||09:15 AM||||Before I bought my 88 Coupe, I drove an 84 Sport Coupe for 4 years.  I thought:<P>On good, smooth pavement, there's not a whole lot of difference in handling between the 88 and 84. But the 88 rides better over rough stuff, and hitting bumps in mid-turn doesn't seem to upset the 88, while the 84 tended to hop over bumps.  If you hit a bump in an offramp, the 84 would jump sideways.  The on-throttle understeer problem was also quite a bit worse with the 84.  The 84 exhibited a lot more nose-dive under braking than the 88.<P>All 84's with manual tranny (except about 7000 Econo Coupes) came with the 4.10 Muncie.  This makes for a pretty quick car off the line.  But after you shift to second, it's pure Iron Duke - slow, steady acceleration.  My 84 would have beaten my 88 to 60 mph though, and probably in the 1/4 mile too.<P>My 84 was mechanically very reliable, but had some electrical problems that my 88 hasn't had (knock on wood): fuel pump, tach filter, two fusible links, three starters in 50K miles.  All of these were inexpensive, but time-consuming - not a big problem in a second car.<P>All in all the 84 was a fun car, but not as satisfying a daily driver as the 88.  But Motorhead's right about the cheap parts.  If you've got the time to invest in this car, why not?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Motorhead||05-30-2000||05:21 PM||||Having said all that, I will admit that the purpose of buying the '84 is for a V8 conversion next year.  Seems the '84 is all by itself when it comes to compatability with the '85 and up V6's and even the later Duke powered cars.<BR>Having bought a new '84 way back when, I recall several warranty trips to the dealer for "electrical" problems."  The '86 however has been flawless since the rebuild. 137,000 miles on the car, 5,000 miles on the engine.  SWEET!<BR>By the way, my first '84 succumbed to the rod through the block syndrome at 62,000 miles, and was treated to a factory fresh Duke courteousy of Pontiac.<P>Thanks<P>Mike||||reg||1
